Carafe bench: a syncpoint event is marked signalled before its kernel event is signalled, and a new wait
clears the kernel event. The host action set the state to Signalling, woke the guest and only then stored
Signalled; libnx's nvFenceWait woke up, freed its event slot and issued the next EventWaitAsync on the same
slot while it was still Signalling, Eden answered BadParameter and NVK reported VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST. A signal
that raced a cancelled wait also stayed on the kernel event and ended the next wait early, so the next wait
on that slot found it still Waiting. On the hardware a wait is armed on a cleared event.
GPL-3.0-or-later, as the file it changes.
diff --git a/src/core/hle/service/nvdrv/devices/nvhost_ctrl.cpp b/src/core/hle/service/nvdrv/devices/nvhost_ctrl.cpp
--- a/src/core/hle/service/nvdrv/devices/nvhost_ctrl.cpp
+++ b/src/core/hle/service/nvdrv/devices/nvhost_ctrl.cpp
@@ -189,2 +189,3 @@ NvResult nvhost_ctrl::IocCtrlEventWait(IocCtrlEventWaitParams& params, bool is_a
 
+    event.kevent->Clear();
     event.status.store(EventState::Waiting, std::memory_order_release);
@@ -203,7 +204,8 @@ NvResult nvhost_ctrl::IocCtrlEventWait(IocCtrlEventWaitParams& params, bool is_a
             auto& event_ = events[slot];
-            if (event_.status.exchange(EventState::Signalling, std::memory_order_acq_rel) ==
-                EventState::Waiting) {
+            const EventState previous =
+                event_.status.exchange(EventState::Signalling, std::memory_order_acq_rel);
+            event_.status.store(EventState::Signalled, std::memory_order_release);
+            if (previous == EventState::Waiting) {
                 event_.kevent->Signal();
             }
-            event_.status.store(EventState::Signalled, std::memory_order_release);
         });
